{
  "rules": [
    "Horizon-dependent bias: days 1–15 are slightly high vs realized (model overestimates by ~0.3–1.5 on average), while days ~22–30 are materially low in rising regimes (model underestimates late-horizon upside by ~2–6+, especially when a breakout occurs).",
    "Breakout/acceleration miss: when the recent history transitions from range-bound to uptrend (e.g., last prices pushing to new 30d highs), the model projects a flat/mean-reverting path and fails to capture trend acceleration; apply an upward drift adjustment that increases with horizon (small near-term, larger after day ~20).",
    "Over-smoothing/lag: the forecast path is too smooth and reacts slowly to regime changes; increase responsiveness by adding a momentum term tied to the last 5–10 day slope (positive slope → lift the whole curve, with stronger lift beyond ~2–3 weeks).",
    "Spike handling asymmetry: after moderate pullbacks, the model often assumes further softness (too bearish around local dips), but realized prices rebound faster; dampen negative extrapolation after a dip and bias slightly upward for the next ~5–10 days if the broader 20–30d trend is up.",
    "Terminal level correction: if the last observed price is near/above the prior 30d high, enforce a higher terminal anchor (e.g., raise days 25–30 toward a continuation scenario) rather than reverting toward the recent mean."
  ]
}
